WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.987
A guardrail is a classifier, so it has error rates.

2
00:00:03.267 --> 00:00:06.104
Week 3 gave you traces of what your system does.

3
00:00:06.384 --> 00:00:09.264
This week is about controlling what it is allowed to do.

4
00:00:09.544 --> 00:00:15.923
A guardrail is any check that sits between a request and a response and can allow, modify, or block it.

5
00:00:16.203 --> 00:00:20.661
Some are deterministic code (a length limit, a regex for card numbers).

6
00:00:20.941 --> 00:00:25.165
Some are models (a moderation endpoint, a small classifier, a judge).

7
00:00:25.445 --> 00:00:40.720
All of them make decisions, and every decision procedure that isn’t trivial has two error rates: it blocks things it should allow (false positives, which annoy and lose real users) and it allows things it should block (false negatives, which are the incidents).

8
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.715
That framing is the most useful idea in this lesson, because it tells you how to work.

9
00:00:45.995 --> 00:00:48.896
You don’t “add guardrails” and declare the system safe.

10
00:00:49.176 --> 00:00:59.224
You decide what you are guarding against, choose a check, and measure both error rates on labeled examples, exactly as you measured LLM judges in Studio 3.

11
00:00:59.504 --> 00:01:07.888
A guard you haven’t measured is a guess, and a guard with a 10 percent false-positive rate on a support bot turns away one customer in ten.

12
00:01:08.168 --> 00:01:09.704
No single layer is enough.

13
00:01:09.984 --> 00:01:15.637
Model providers train their models to decline harmful requests and run their own classifiers.

14
00:01:15.917 --> 00:01:29.251
You add checks on the input, checks on the output, and, most important, an architecture that limits damage when everything else fails: least privilege for tools, no secrets in prompts, approval before side effects.

15
00:01:29.531 --> 00:01:31.941
Lesson 11 covers that last layer.

16
00:01:32.221 --> 00:01:34.056
This lesson builds the first ones.
